Ladybird takes a box to be the root element’s box, and so the one that
paints the canvas background, only if its element is the document
element. Blink’s LayoutObject::IsDocumentElement() and WebKit’s
RenderObject::isDocumentElementRenderer() compare the object’s node
with the document’s documentElement, and Gecko’s nsCSSRendering::
FindBackgroundFrame() takes the root element’s frame from the frame
constructor’s GetRootElementStyleFrame(). An html element anywhere
else is an ordinary element in all three.

Chrome 157.0.8103.78, Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5 all paint a page
with one html element in a div, and another in a shadow root, just like
the same page without them. And Ladybird now does as well.
